# Git commits — proactive, small, user-only

After every meaningful chunk of work (feature, fix, or polish step), **commit and push immediately**. Do not wait for the user to say "commit" or "push".

## When to commit + push

- After finishing a logical step (e.g. one Phase 3 feature, one file group, one bug fix)
- Before moving to the next task in the same session
- When the user says "commit" — that means **commit AND push**
- When the user might stop or context might end — commit and push first

## How to commit

- **Small commits** with clear messages (e.g. `feat:`, `fix:`, `style:`)
- Use `git commit-tree` / `hash-object` on Windows if regular `git commit` adds `Co-authored-by: Cursor`
- Never add `Co-authored-by: Cursor` — commits are credited only to **jai tripathi**
- Git path on Windows: `C:\Program Files\Git\cmd\git.exe`
- **Always push** to the current feature branch after committing (`git push origin HEAD`)

## Do not

- Leave completed work uncommitted at end of turn
- Batch many unrelated changes into one giant commit
- Commit secrets (`.env`, credentials)
